### E21. Why-probe: why the critic values a raised tail (2026-10-30)

- **Question.** Is the tail angle (A) a stand-in for the episode phase, or (B) a real sign of termination risk? [measured, RUNLOG l.803]
- **Design.** No policy training. Predictions hashed before data. stance6 s42 at 1.05M and 2.10M; BS2 controls at 3.15M and the end; a blinded run. A verifier and an adjudicator [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804].
- **Result.** "(A) PHASE PROXY favoured strongly [measured inputs, inferred reading]; (B) genuine termination risk EXCLUDED" [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
- **Key numbers.** [all measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - The 63 observations have no clock. Four stance reward terms are paid only after step 200 of each episode. [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - Settled states are worth 0.17-0.23 more (normalised return-to-go) at matched posture. [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - The policy's own tail command creeps up over about 300 steps after each reset. So the tail angle tells the time (AUC 0.61-0.95). [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - The phase-blind critic overvalues steps 50-200 by +0.04 to +0.14. It puts +0.02 to +0.06 per degree on the tail. [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - Real returns give the tail -0.013 to -0.002 per degree once phase is controlled. [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - Verifier's fitted critic: +0.048 to +0.068 per degree without the phase; -0.006 to +0.004 with it. The training's own advantages push the tail up; Monte Carlo advantages do not detectably. [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - Termination: 0 tail contacts in 1,280 training-like rollouts and in 2,495 training episodes. [measured, RUNLOG l.803-804]
  - Post hoc: s80's neck shows the same clock signature toward its own habit (one checkpoint). [measured, RUNLOG l.804; post hoc, one checkpoint]
- **Status.** Final, verified [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
- **Record.** RUNLOG l.803-804. S2 `com_revisit/notes/{phase_probe,termination_probe,verify_why}`. Safety copy `handoff-day-2026-10-30` [measured, RUNLOG l.807]. Records draft (E26 below).

