## 2. What we now know: the causal story

The story has four links. Each is labelled with how strong its evidence is.

### Link 0: the habit itself

- On r9 and r10, with the stance recipe, tail_3's command climbs to +1 in some seeds and stays there [measured, E1-E5, E12]. The same actuator and sign every time on the tail: tail_3, tail tip up [measured, RUNLOG l.228, l.266, l.460, l.711].
- It is common. 4 of 10 r9 L+T-family runs railed [measured, RUNLOG l.613]. In stance6, 1 of 3 railed and 1 parked [measured, RUNLOG l.711].
- It costs reward. Holding tail_3 at 0 earns more: +54.6 and +36.2 per window under the policy's own noise, +63.6 in the clamp test [measured, RUNLOG l.526, l.750].
- It is not forced by the body. The plant does not push the tail up [measured, RUNLOG l.508]. The raised tail moves the centre of mass by at most 0.24 mm fore-aft [measured, RUNLOG l.746]. No tail contact ends episodes [measured, RUNLOG l.804].

### Link 1: four stance reward terms switch on at step 200 (measured)

- Four stance terms (stance width, lateral lean, stagger, CoP band) are paid only after `stance_width_settle_steps` = 200 [measured, RUNLOG l.803, code at 8737cd3d].
- The robot's 63 observations contain no clock [measured, RUNLOG l.803].
- So the same pose is worth more after step 200 than before it: 0.17-0.23 more return-to-go at matched posture [measured, RUNLOG l.804]. On the run branch the value jumps +0.31 to +0.35 per step at step 200 [measured, RUNLOG l.810].

### Link 2: the critic uses the creeping tail_3 angle as a clock (measured inputs, inferred reading)

- The policy's tail command creeps up over about 300 steps after every reset. So the tail angle tracks the step count (AUC 0.61-0.95) [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
- The critic, which has no clock, learns to read the tail angle. Without the phase it puts +0.02 to +0.06 per degree on the tail; real returns put -0.013 to -0.002 per degree on it once phase is known [measured, RUNLOG l.804]. A fitted critic gives +0.048 to +0.068 per degree without the phase and about 0 with it [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
- The critic's errors have the expected timing: too high before step 200, too low just after it [measured, RUNLOG l.804, l.861].
- Termination risk does not explain the slope; it could fund at most 7% of it [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
- That the tail angle works as a clock, rather than as anything else, is the adjudicated reading [inferred, RUNLOG l.804: "measured inputs, inferred reading"].

### Link 3: the policy gradient pays for raising the tail (measured from 2.10M; unresolved at the onset)

- Only the policy-gradient term moves tail_3's mean. The entropy and value terms push it by exactly 0 [measured, RUNLOG l.770-771]. This agrees with ECS: removing entropy changed nothing [measured, RUNLOG l.740].
- The training's advantages push the tail up. Monte Carlo advantages (real rewards only) do not detectably [measured, RUNLOG l.804]. The critic-bootstrap pull is measured from 2.10M (t 3.7) and 3.15M (t 7.9) [measured, RUNLOG l.771].
- At the onset (1.31M in s42) an upward pull exists, but whether the critic causes it there is unresolved (+0.0005 ± 0.00045) [measured, RUNLOG l.771].
- Once raised, the tail stays raised through a positive loop: the policy answers a higher observed tail with a higher command (loop gain about 0.63 at the s42 ends), and the fixed point lies past +1 [measured, RUNLOG l.750, l.527]. Body posture feeds this loop in s42 (nose-down maps to tail-up) [measured, RUNLOG l.757].
- So the rail is the loop's fixed point, and the critic's clock error is what first tilts the loop upward [inferred, from the measured items above].

### Link 4: a critic-only phase input removes it (measured in two screens and one round; not confirmed by the pre-registered round test)

- Taking the tail angle away from the critic cut the climb: BS2, p 1/70 [measured, RUNLOG l.794]. But blinding is a stopgap: the phase can be read from other joints (AUC 0.84-0.99), and one blinded run's tail_2 rose [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
- Telling the critic the phase, with the tail angle still visible, cut it too: PS, p 1/70. The critic's own tail slope fell to about 0, and its errors around step 200 nearly vanished [measured, RUNLOG l.857, l.861; PS `REPORT.md` lines 39-41].
- From scratch on Colab, 0 of 3 PHASE runs railed or parked, and none formed a habit on another joint. Their tail drive was 13-49 times lower than the same seeds without the input [measured, `score_pc.txt` lines 321-343, 357, 362].
- The OFF runs equal stance6 bit for bit, so each same-seed pair differs only by the phase input [measured that they are equal: RUNLOG l.970-971; inferred that only the input differs: RUNLOG l.970].
- The s80 neck habit, present with the switch off, was absent with it on [measured, `score_pc.txt` lines 329, 342]. The why-probe had seen a neck clock in s80 [measured, RUNLOG l.804]. That the same mechanism drives the neck habit is a reading [inferred].
- The limits stand. The pinned test is NOT SEPARATED (p = 20/20) [measured, `score_pc.txt` line 362]. The deviation reading (p = 1/20) is post hoc [measured, RUNLOG l.969]. With 3 seeds, 1/20 is the smallest p possible, and no rail in 3 runs would happen by chance 30% of the time [measured, `score_pc.txt` line 359]. The reading is "consistent with", "not confirmatory" [measured, `score_pc.txt` line 357].

### What was ruled out or did not work along the way

- Not supported as the cause: the plant forcing the pin [measured, RUNLOG l.508]; a static counterweight need [measured, RUNLOG l.746]; a termination risk [measured, RUNLOG l.804]; normaliser drift, which opposes the loop [measured, RUNLOG l.757]; the entropy bonus, which does not push the mean and made no difference from mid-rise states (its role in the early phase was not tested) [measured, RUNLOG l.740, l.742, l.771].
- Did not fix it: the #504 reward ramp on r9 [measured, TAILREC line 1311]; P1 (not established) [measured, RUNLOG l.438]; BOUND90 (rail kept) and BOUND60 (rail turned into a park) [measured, RUNLOG l.573]; physics r10 (rail in 1 of 3, park in 1 of 3) [measured, RUNLOG l.711].
- Not a usable warning: the probe bank, and the loop gain across runs [measured, RUNLOG l.528, l.769, l.773].

---

## 3. What is still open

1. **No confirmatory test yet.** The pinned ONE TEST is NOT SEPARATED; only the flagged deviation reads p = 1/20 [measured, `score_pc.txt` line 362; RUNLOG l.969]. A confirmatory round needs new seeds. The OFF arm here is stance6 again [measured, RUNLOG l.970].
2. **Determinism changes the design options.** Same seed, same code, same Colab image gave identical runs [measured, RUNLOG l.970-971]. A future round could pair by seed exactly and reuse past runs as controls [inferred]. How far this holds (other images, other configs) is unknown.
3. **The onset's cause.** The critic-bootstrap pull is measured from 2.10M but unresolved at the onset (1.2-1.3M) [measured, RUNLOG l.771]. BS from 1.05M was not informative [measured, RUNLOG l.779]. PC's from-scratch runs never rose at all, which covers the onset only in 3 runs [measured, `score_pc.txt` lines 322, 326, 330].
4. **The posture pathway.** s42's networks turn nose-down pitch into tail-up; s60 has the opposite sensitivity [measured, RUNLOG l.757]. How this fits the critic-clock story is not tested [inferred].
5. **PHASE s60's 9 evaluation falls** in 910 episodes (others 0-1) are unexplained [measured, `score_pc.txt` line 324].
6. **No parent named.** The K1-K4 judge was not run at the PC finals [measured, `score_pc.txt` line 361].
7. **Beyond stance.** Whether the phase input works in walking stages, where there is no step-200 gate, is untested [inferred].
8. **The other fix.** Making the reward stationary (removing the step-200 gate) was judged "only partial" by the why-probe and was never tried [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
9. **`main` versus the run branch.** `main` gates one term at step 200, the run branch four; the step-200 jump is about 3.5 times smaller on `main` [measured, RUNLOG l.809-810]. Whether `main`'s stance recipe shows the habit at the same rate is not measured.
10. **Merge or not.** The critic-only phase input lives only on `screen-trex-r10-phasecritic` at 1dfe2327, not on `main` [measured, RUNLOG l.879].
11. **The neck habit.** The s80 neck "clock" was seen at one checkpoint only [measured, RUNLOG l.804].
12. **Small drift on other tail joints.** In PS the phase arm's tail_2 command was a little higher (p 2/70) [measured, RUNLOG l.857]. In PC no other tail joint parked [measured, `score_pc.txt` lines 323, 327, 331]. Longer runs of a different design were never made (LB never run, LP abandoned) [measured, RUNLOG l.805, l.868].
13. **No sham-input arm.** PS had no arm with a meaningless extra critic input, so its critic-slope result is not separated from a tail that simply climbed less [measured, PS `REPORT.md` line 107]. PC had none either: its OFF runs add no input at all [measured, `ST/round/criteria.txt` lines 86-94].
14. **Other runs and states.** PS tested one start state (s42 at 2.10M); PC tested 3 seeds on one recipe [measured, PS `REPORT.md` line 103; `score_pc.txt` line 357]. Whether the input holds on `main`'s configuration (one gated term) is untested [measured, PS `REPORT.md` line 106].
15. **Bounds on r10.** P1, BOUND90 and BOUND60 were screened on r9 only [measured, TAILREC lines 1314-1316].
16. **Records.** PC has no records PR yet. The draft `records-phase-screen` is unpushed and holds a placeholder for this round [measured, RUNLOG l.871-872].

